In [[W:World-systems theory|world-systems theory]], '''core countries''' (alternatively '''core states''' or the '''imperial core''') are the industrialized, developed nations that command the most influence over the global political and economic system. Core countries possess advanced industrial capacity, powerful militaries, strong state institutions, and the ability to shape international institutions, alliances, and norms. They exercise substantial control or influence over global resource flows, trade networks, and political outcomes.

Unlike theoretical frameworks developed in other world-systems that associated core status exclusively with [[W:Capitalism|capitalist political economy]], the concept of the core countries recognizes that both capitalist and [[W:Socialism|socialist]] developed states can occupy core positions based on industrial capacity, military power, technological advancement, and global political influence. The emergence of socialist core states—most prominently [[Balisca]], [[Burawa]], and [[Daras]]—following the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] (1904–1905) and [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918–1922) combined with the acceleration of [[W:Decolonization|decolonization]] after the [[Great Adonian War]] has created a [[W:Multipolarity|multipolar]] world order characterized by competing systems rather than a unified core exploiting a unified periphery.

Core countries do not permanently retain their status. Throughout Adonian history, core countries have changed, with states rising to and falling from core status based on internal development, external competition, and systemic transformations. The contemporary multi-polar system emerged from the integration of socialist great powers into the world-system during the early 20th century, fundamentally altering the dynamics of international relations.

== Definition and characteristics ==

Core countries control and benefit substantially from the Adonian socio-economic and political system. They possess:

* '''Industrial and technological capacity:''' Core states maintain advanced industrial bases capable of producing manufactured goods, technology, and military equipment at scale.
* '''Military power:''' Core states deploy military capabilities sufficient for self-defense, power projection, and enforcement of international commitments.
* '''State capacity:''' Effective governance institutions—whether centralized nation-states, confederal assemblies, or other forms—enable core states to mobilize resources, maintain internal order, and conduct foreign policy.
* '''Population welfare:''' Core populations experience relatively high standards of living, including life expectancy, education, health care, and economic security, achieved through varying mechanisms depending on political-economic system.
* '''International influence:''' Core states shape international institutions, alliances, trade networks, and normative frameworks.

The core is distinguished from the [[Semi-periphery (world-systems theory)|semi-periphery]]—states possessing some but not all core characteristics—and the [[Periphery (world-systems theory)|periphery]]—states with limited industrial development, weaker state capacity, and less international influence.

